# --- research archive ------------------------------------------------------
# Raw collector responses: kept locally for replaying a parsing bug, but not
# worth the repository weight. Everything derived from them lives in
# data/papers/ and data/videos/, which ARE tracked.
data/raw/

# Run logs (also covered by *.log above).
data/logs/

# PDFs filed by hand: the inbox they are dropped into, and the store they are
# moved to. Heavy, rarely ours to redistribute, and always obtainable again
# from wherever they came from. The RECORD of each one is tracked in
# data/papers/, so the archive stays complete in a fresh clone even though the
# bytes do not travel with it.
inbox/*.pdf
inbox/**/*.pdf
# Covers data/pdfs/read/ too -- documents whose reading is done are shelved
# there rather than in a directory of their own, precisely so that no
# deployment can ignore one and forget the other.
data/pdfs/

# Announced-paper abstracts. This deployment takes the trade the sweep offers:
# the coverage ledger in data/index/ IS committed, so the per-day audit of what
# arXiv announced against what we hold survives a fresh clone, but the abstract
# text itself does not -- it is roughly 1.5 KB per paper and would grow the
# repository by ~100 MB a year across four categories.
#
# The consequence is stated plainly: a fresh clone starts with a large recorded
# debt and re-fetches abstracts at one request per paper, worst day first. That
# is the ledger working, not failing. Un-ignore this if the crawling cost ever
# matters more than the weight.
data/abstracts/

# NOTE: data/index/seen.sqlite is intentionally NOT ignored. Scheduled runs
# start from a fresh clone, so the deduplication state has to be committed or
# every run re-processes everything it has already seen.

# --- migration -------------------------------------------------------------
# The staging folder for a move between environments. Its payload is the
# gitignored half of the archive -- PDFs, abstracts, raw responses, logs -- so
# committing it here would defeat every reason those are ignored in the first
# place. The instructions are tracked, because the environment that has to read
# them is the one that has only just cloned this repository.
/migration/*
!/migration/README.md
